<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cecil Rhodes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1853–1902</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cecil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> was a British businessman and politician in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> who made a fortune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> diamonds through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> and in gold. Prime Minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> from 1890 to 1896, he used his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-south-africa-company">British South Africa Company</a> to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashonaland">Mashonaland</a> in 1890 and conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> kingdom in 1893, lands named Rhodesia after him. His part in the failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jameson-raid">Jameson Raid</a> of 1895 forced his resignation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes.jpg" alt="Cecil Rhodes" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_John_Rhodes_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
